  "root": {
    "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
    "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-ceb741eed8@example.invalid",
    "jcr:lastModified": "Fri Aug 23 2019 18:13:48 GMT+0000",
    "sling:resourceType": "wcm/foundation/components/responsivegrid",
    "text": {
      "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
      "jcr:createdBy": "user-73104d59b2@example.invalid",
      "topPadding": "3",
      "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-73104d59b2@example.invalid",
      "hideComponent": "false",
      "jcr:created": "Wed Jun 10 2026 16:10:42 GMT+0000",
      "gradientDirection": "left-to-right",
      "text": "<p>Labor and delivery departments and operating rooms are hectic by nature. Patient safety is of the highest priority, yet challenges and unintended outcomes like <a href=\"https://www.jointcommission.org/en-us/knowledge-library/newsletters/sentinel-event-alert/issue-51\">retained surgical sponges</a> and <a href=\"https://www.mayoclinic.org/medical-professionals/obstetrics-gynecology/news/postpartum-hemorrhage-risks-and-current-management/mac-20533920\">postpartum hemorrhage</a> (PPH) can happen. In fact, 88% of retained surgical items are due to incorrect counts.¹ And nationwide, PPH is an issue on the rise.²</p>\n<p>Nakeisha McNeill Tolliver, AORN Past President 2024-2025 and Director of Perioperative Services at Texas Children's Hospital, says many times staff members don't know they have a problem until it shows up and rears its head.³</p>\n",
      "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Jun 10 2026 16:51:55 GMT+0000",
      "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
      "textIsRich": "true",
      "cq:responsive": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "default": {
          "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
          "offset": "1",
          "width": "10"
        },
        "phone": {
          "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
          "offset": "0",
          "width": "12"
        }
      }
    },
    "text_1021029965": {
      "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
      "jcr:createdBy": "user-73104d59b2@example.invalid",
      "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-73104d59b2@example.invalid",
      "jcr:created": "Wed Jun 10 2026 16:12:23 GMT+0000",
      "text": "<p>“Despite the fact that we come to work every day expecting to just do the most excellent cases and provide the best care we possibly can, human error happens,” she said.</p>\n<p>The manual counting process can be cumbersome and could lead to a retained surgical sponge—a potentially life-threatening situation. Retained surgical items (RSIs) are considered a sentinel event by The Joint Commission.⁴ Sentinel events are considered preventable, serious, and should theoretically never occur.</p>\n<p>When it comes to PPH, care teams might not know if a patient is in danger if they don’t know how much blood has been lost, and it could be too late when they do find out. In fact, there are 50,000 cases of severe maternal morbidity annually nationwide.⁵</p>\n<p>Both retained surgical sponges and unidentified PPH can be debilitating for patients and reduce trust in healthcare providers. That’s why the right equipment to help count surgical sponges and assess blood loss is crucial.</p>\n",
      "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Jun 10 2026 16:51:49 GMT+0000",
      "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
      "textIsRich": "true",
      "cq:responsive": {
