    "text_1366105363": {
      "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
      "jcr:createdBy": "user-73104d59b2@example.invalid",
      "jcr:title": "Whether",
      "cq:masterBuildingBlock": true,
      "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-73104d59b2@example.invalid",
      "jcr:created": "Wed Jun 10 2026 16:44:56 GMT+0000",
      "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Jun 10 2026 16:46:53 GMT+0000",
      "sling:resourceType": "cq/experience-fragments/editor/components/buildingblock",
      "text_1366105363129338770777967": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-73104d59b2@example.invalid",
        "text": "<p>Whether the work happens in an operating room or labor and delivery department, adjunct technology exists to help address some challenges faced during procedures. With SurgiCount+ and its integrated Triton technology, you can feel confident in your surgical sponge count and blood loss assessment.</p>\n<p>Hospital leadership might question whether adjunct technology will disrupt staff members, but Tolliver explains it’s meant to give an extra vote of confidence. Stryker’s product can be seen as an investment in patient safety and caregiver confidence. Plus, Stryker has an <a href=\"https://www.aorn.org/education/education-for-facilities/surgical-safety-center-of-excellence/retained-surgical-items-rsi\">AORN Center of Excellence in Surgical Safety: RSI Prevention</a> certification.</p>\n<p>“As all hospitals work through trying to understand how we make surgery safer, the adjunct technology is what will help with that,” said Tolliver. “I think it's important to know that it is a part of the counting process, and it doesn't remove the manual [counting].”</p>\n",
        "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Jun 10 2026 16:46:23 GMT+0000",
        "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
        "textIsRich": "true",
